    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d184">

                <pb facs="0540" n="1170" sameAs="frus:frus1873p1v2/pg1170"/>

                <head>No. 490.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Pile to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States of America</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Caracas</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1872-09-23"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >September</hi> 23, 1872</date>. (Rec’d Oct. 21.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 64.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The steamer Virginius left Puerto Cabello
                    on the evening of September 7, dispatched in the usual manner by the United
                    States consul and local authorities of that place. She passed out very near the
                    Spanish man-of-war Basco Nuñez de Balboa, and went to sea without a shot being
                    fired at her.</p>
                <p>Thus has ended the “bluster and noise” that has been kept up for four months at
                    Puerto Cabello about this vessel.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WM. A. PILE.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
